# Language policy

## After prompt87 Phase B (2026-07-22)

| Layer | Language | Examples |
|-------|----------|----------|
| **Language gate + Quick Start** | **`appLocale`** (`userLanguage`) | Wizard copy, units row labels, help `?` → `/{lang}/help/` |
| **Dashboard chrome** | **`appLocale`** | Strip titles, Food Log, Profile nested labels, status/ago, dates |
| **Coach & meals & exports** | **`appLocale`** | Gemini chat, meal `name_local`, visit reports |
| **Clinic portal UI** | **`clinicLocale`** (10 languages, be-26) | Global clinics — reversed 2026-07-26 |
| **Website help** | **Path locale** | `https://healthings.ai/{en\|he\|es\|fr\|de\|ar\|ru}/help/…` |

## Always-English glossary

Brand, units (`kcal`, `mg/dL`, `kg`), acronyms (`CGM`, `BMR`, `AI`), device names (Withings…), slash commands. Profile nested titles use bare nouns (Profile / Targets…), not “My …” product phrases.

## Settings UX

One language preference drives coach, meals, Quick Start, help links, and dashboard chrome. Prefer **App & coach language** when touching the Profile picker label.

## RTL

Hebrew/Arabic: RTL for Quick Start and user-content areas (chat, meals, exports). Dashboard strip structure stays LTR chrome with localized strings; full layout RTL is deferred.

## Intent detection

Chat slash commands and natural-language triggers may stay multilingual (`chatIntent.ts`).

## Clinic portal (Phase C — triggered 2026-07-26)

Owner decision: **Healthings is global, and so is the clinic product.** English-only was a
clinician-tool assumption; a clinic in Madrid or Tel Aviv is exactly the customer. The portal ships
the **same 10 languages as the app** (`en he es fr de ar ru pt it tr` — `SUPPORTED_LANGUAGES`), not
the 8 the help site carries.

### Locale is per account, never per link

**A patient reading Hebrew may be treated by a clinic reading English.** `appLocale` and
`clinicLocale` are independent; neither side's choice changes the other's UI. Do not derive one from
the other, and do not store a language on `account_shares`.

| Layer | Language |
|----|----|
| Portal chrome (login, worklist, actions, flash) | `clinicLocale` |
| Clinical vocabulary in the workspace | `clinicLocale`; keep the always-English glossary |
| Patient-authored content (meal names, rules text, chat) | **Unchanged** — shown as the patient wrote it |

Because the two mix on one screen, patient-authored text needs an explicit `dir`, and **which one
depends on the field**:

| Content | `dir` | Why |
|----|----|----|
| Free-form prose — meal names, patient rules, chat | `auto` | Let the first strong character pick the base direction |
| **Email addresses**, URLs, IDs, numbers+units | **`ltr`** | The syntax is LTR |

`dir="auto"` on `דנה.לוי@example.co.il` resolves the whole string to RTL and paints the domain to the
*left* of the local part — it reads as corrupted data, not as Hebrew. `dir="ltr"` keeps `local@domain`
in order while the Hebrew run still shapes right-to-left inside it. Verified in the be-25 RTL probe.

### Portal RTL and copy storage

Hebrew/Arabic get `dir="rtl"` on the portal, which is new: the app defers full chrome RTL, the portal
does not, because a table flips cleanly where the dashboard strip does not.

Copy lives in a catalog keyed by locale — same shape as `app/src/i18n/*Copy.ts` and
`HELP_LOCALES` — never inline strings in the page.

### Clinic-written text reaching a patient in another language — open

An English clinic's `rules_json` reaches a Hebrew patient verbatim through `pullClinicOverlays`.
A string catalog cannot fix this: translating a clinical directive is **Gemini judgment**
(`ai-judgment-not-regex.mdc`), not a lookup. Needs an owner decision — original, translation, or
both — and its own batch. Do not silently machine-translate a dietary instruction.

## When to revisit

Full app chrome RTL if Hebrew/Arabic users need mirrored strip layout.
